{align:justify|In Liaozhong County, Liaoning Province, northeastern China, on an early-autumn morning in 1997, a gentle breeze blew; the Liao River lay calm, its water limpid green, grasses lush along its banks, branches swaying as the light wind passed. All was warm and gentle under the morning sun.}
{align:justify|Mr. Do, rod in hand and basket on his shoulder, strode briskly to the riverbank. He baited his hook and cast it in, then stared fixedly at the water, waiting in suspense, his face at once tense and delighted. Something like a big fish seemed to have taken the bait — the taut line and bending rod said so — and he jerked the line up. As the hooked creature broke the surface, Mr. Do's face shifted from joy to astonishment at the strange sight before him — one he could say he had never witnessed in all his years of fishing.}
{align:justify|The creature on the hook was no big fish but a great mother turtle. Clinging to her shell were several baby turtles, gripping their mother fast — so all were hauled up together. The moment the line touched ground, the little turtles acted like bodyguards: they ringed the great turtle at once, tiny black eyes glaring at Mr. Do, full of alarm. Bold and calm all his life, before this uncanny scene he did not know what to do.}
{align:justify|In truth he had heard tales of animals' spiritual nature, but such rumors and hearsay had never concerned him, nor had he believed a word — he always supposed people loved to exaggerate and invent.}
{align:justify|But now, facing this vivid yet unbelievable sight, he was utterly conquered. These tiny turtles reminded him of his own childhood, of the deep love of parents and siblings. And he thought these little turtles must be the same — knowing love and longing to protect their own. Surely they clung to their mother and loved her just as he had as a child. So the animals that humans slaughter without mercy also possess spirit, feeling, and lives just like our own.}
{align:justify|Mr. Do's conscience awoke, and at last he resolved to do something he had never done in his life: release captive animals! Quietly he unhooked the great turtle and set her in the water, then freed the baby turtles one by one after her, his heart full of remorse. He said to the turtles:}
{align:justify|"Forgive me — go home safely now!"}
{align:justify|The little turtles swam after their mother, and one baby kept turning back to look at him.}
{align:justify|Afterward Mr. Do snapped his fishing rod in two. From that day he never fished again and wholly gave up killing. He is now a faithful disciple of Buddhism, often retelling this story and urging everyone never to harm living beings.}
{align:justify|As the proverb says: "Man's nature at birth is fundamentally good." Our original nature is kind — but as the years grow and under the sway of unwholesome surroundings, that goodness is gradually veiled, and before we know it we have sunk into wrongdoing.}
{align:justify|The fortunate meet the conditions that wake them — reforming, abandoning evil for good, and recovering their own pure nature. Mr. Do is one of those fortunate ones.}
